--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
UPSTREAM ATTRIBUTION THAT TRAVELS WITH THESE FILES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every row in every table here carries its own source in a `source_key` column,
resolved in SOURCES.md, and -- for the lattice-constant table -- its own DOI
naming the original structure determination. Those citations travel with the
values; the grant above does not replace them.

  * Crystallography Open Database (CC0 1.0) -- the lattice-constant values.
    COD dedicates its data to the public domain but asks that users acknowledge
    the original authors of each structure determination, which is why every row
    carries its own DOI, author list and COD entry id.
    https://www.crystallography.net/cod/

  * NASA Glenn Research Center -- the atomic-oxygen erosion yields.
    K. K. de Groh, B. A. Banks, C. E. McCarthy, R. N. Rucker, L. M. Roberts,
    L. A. Berger, "MISSE PEACE Polymers Atomic Oxygen Erosion Results",
    NASA/TM-2006-214482 (2006). https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20070002707
    A US Government work; not subject to copyright in the United States.

  * Materials Project (CC BY 4.0) -- material ids in materials.csv, used only as
    join keys. No Materials Project VALUE is used as reference data anywhere in
    this collection; see SOURCES.md for why.
    A. Jain et al., APL Materials 1, 011002 (2013). doi:10.1063/1.4812323

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
EXCLUSIONS -- third-party material NOT licensed by this file
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The following are not this project's to license. They are excluded from the
CC BY 4.0 grant above and keep their own status:

  reference_data/raw/NASA-TM-2006-214482_MISSE2_PEACE.pdf
      The NASA technical memorandum itself, vendored verbatim so that the
      extraction is reproducible offline, with its sha256 recorded alongside.
      A US Government work, in the public domain in the United States. The NASA
      insignia and logotype are NOT in the public domain and must not be reused
      separately; NASA's use of any third-party material inside the document
      conveys no rights to others.

  reference_data/raw/cod/*.json
      Cached responses from the Crystallography Open Database, stored verbatim
      so that the build reproduces without network access. CC0 1.0 (public
      domain dedication), Copyright waived by the COD depositors; the original
      structure authors are credited per row as described above.

See reference_data/SOURCES.md for the licence of every source, including the
sources that were examined and rejected, and why.
